Z||000000||AutoXdbdragSE||10-11-2001||11:22 PM||||Ok i've looked on the internet and all the performance numbers are the 10.9 from the standard transmision..  <BR>i fail to believe with the more agresive ratio it is the same 10.9<BR>I've clocked mine @ 9.5ish with the good ol stopwatch method.. but i've got a couple.. "performace/goodbye most emisions crap mods"  so does anyone know the actual numbers for this type of fiero.. also weight would be nice.  Oh and i'm going to be rebuilding the suspension on my car for autocross. the rules state i can use any options offered on the 84 se.. would it be my advantage to run 13" rims with lo-pro racing tires.. or keep the 14" rims with lo-pros.. anyone with autocross expierience in a fiero please help.. thanks alot<P>------------------<BR>Bahh.. V-6, who needs it.. i got me some 4.10's||||reg||1
Z||000001||Master_Sushi||10-12-2001||12:06 AM||||Im assuming the 84 se 4.10's are mid 9 sec 0-60. My auto 88 coupe coupe hit 60 in under 10 secs, 88s had a bit more power. But driving my 84, it seems faster them my 88 coupe did. Really hammering the gears I could probably get low 9's 0-60. Its funny I've never really done a burn out in a fiero tell recently. But reving to 3K and leting the clutch go around corners sure is fun! I cant beleave what fun i've been missing. (if I keep up all this fun, soon ill have fun puting in a new clutch  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> lol) I will NEVER buy an automatic fiero again. I love the low gearing of the muncie.<P>I have K&N and I think an upgraded exhaust on my 84se. If it dosent have an upgraded enhaust I dont know whats going on. It has a really high pitched ricer sound and my coupe sounded more like a low pitched rumble. Its quite zippy.<P>I would think that youd be better off with low profile 14 inch wheels over 13 inchers. More contact with the road. Won't the 14's be a little less heavy since they are (sp?)alluminum?<P>Anyway, I had a fun day spending about 90% of it working on my fiero! So im happy...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000002||Jefrysuko||10-12-2001||01:50 AM||||I would say that the best thing for you to do would be get rid on those tire barn specials and get some real tires  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  Seriously, I priced BFG Comp T/A HR4's at Sam's club for $59 a piece and they are rated really well.  If you go with a racing tire won't that put you in a different class?||||reg||1
Z||000003||tomcat06085||10-12-2001||01:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B>I would say that the best thing for you to do would be get rid on those tire barn specials and get some real tires   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  Seriously, I priced BFG Comp T/A HR4's at Sam's club for $59 a piece and they are rated really well.  If you go with a racing tire won't that put you in a different class?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>In Solo or autocross classification, DOT legal tires are allowed in stock and street prepared classes. I've seen the faster, more experienced guys using the 15/205 Kuhmo racing tires and still allowed to run in stock class. They are generally running for FTD or winning their class, unless it's filled up with other Fiero's. It's great to see the stock Fiero's running all over the vettes and bmw's and other expensive hotrods. Of course, the mr2's  are in there making it tuff on the Fiero's.<P><P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Help me go fast!||||reg||14
